1
00:00:02,885 --> 00:00:30,952
[SPEAKER_00]: So, yeah, tonight's, tonight's sutta is Angotirinikāya 1062, titled Tanhāra Craving, and says, the Buddha talks to the monks and says, because this is said, no first point of craving for being is evident, before which there was no craving for being, bhāvatāna,

2
00:00:31,735 --> 00:00:33,696
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, that afterwards then came to be.

3
00:00:34,537 --> 00:00:39,080
[SPEAKER_00]: Still, eh... it is evident that craving for being depends on this.

4
00:00:40,060 --> 00:00:45,424
[SPEAKER_00]: In other words, eh... there is no first point of craving, but that doesn't mean that it doesn't depend on anything.

5
00:00:45,864 --> 00:00:47,425
[SPEAKER_00]: That it's not sustained by anything.

6
00:00:48,686 --> 00:00:53,108
[SPEAKER_00]: And then the Buddha says, eh... he says, I say that the craving for being has its nutriment.

7
00:00:53,329 --> 00:00:54,469
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not without nutriment.

8
00:00:55,170 --> 00:01:00,113
[SPEAKER_00]: And the nutriment, eh... for, eh... craving for being is ignorance or avijjā.

9
00:01:02,467 --> 00:01:07,811
[SPEAKER_00]: I say that ignorance has a nutriment and it's not without a nutriment.

10
00:01:08,391 --> 00:01:11,333
[SPEAKER_00]: The nutriment for ignorance is the five hindrances.

11
00:01:13,615 --> 00:01:15,576
[SPEAKER_00]: I say that the five hindrances have the nutriment.

12
00:01:15,796 --> 00:01:17,037
[SPEAKER_00]: They're not without the nutriment.

13
00:01:17,397 --> 00:01:21,660
[SPEAKER_00]: The nutriment for five hindrances are three kinds of misconduct.

14
00:01:23,962 --> 00:01:27,044
[SPEAKER_00]: I say that the three kinds of misconduct have the nutriment.

15
00:01:27,244 --> 00:01:28,445
[SPEAKER_00]: They're not without the nutriment.

16
00:01:29,829 --> 00:01:33,352
[SPEAKER_00]: And the nutriment for the three kinds of misconduct is the lack of sense-restraint.

17
00:01:34,914 --> 00:01:36,895
[SPEAKER_00]: I say that the lack of sense-restraint has its nutriment.

18
00:01:37,176 --> 00:01:38,217
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not without a nutriment.

19
00:01:38,997 --> 00:01:43,081
[SPEAKER_00]: And the nutriment for the lack of sense-restraint is the lack of recollection and awareness.

20
00:01:43,101 --> 00:01:43,942
[SPEAKER_00]: Sati, sampajañña.

21
00:01:46,089 --> 00:01:54,972
[SPEAKER_00]: I say that the lack of recollection and awareness has its nutriment, and it's not without the nutriment, and that nutriment is attention not in the light of its source.

22
00:01:55,612 --> 00:02:08,896
[SPEAKER_00]: How we translate the yoniso manasikāra, the womb attention, or the attention with the origin, or wise attention, doesn't matter how you translate it, basically, it needs to be understood.

23
00:02:09,036 --> 00:02:13,517
[SPEAKER_00]: I say that the attention not in the light of its source has its nutriment, it's not without the nutriment.

24
00:02:14,484 --> 00:02:16,565
[SPEAKER_00]: What is the nutriment for yoniso manasikāra?

25
00:02:16,885 --> 00:02:18,246
[SPEAKER_00]: And it's the lack of faith.

26
00:02:20,527 --> 00:02:22,408
[SPEAKER_00]: I say that the lack of faith has its nutriment.

27
00:02:22,788 --> 00:02:23,888
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not without nutriment.

28
00:02:24,209 --> 00:02:25,469
[SPEAKER_00]: What's the nutriment for the lack of faith?

29
00:02:26,089 --> 00:02:27,810
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not listening to the true teaching.

30
00:02:30,571 --> 00:02:32,913
[SPEAKER_00]: I say that not listening to the true teaching has its nutriment.

31
00:02:33,133 --> 00:02:34,273
[SPEAKER_00]: And it's not without the nutriment.

32
00:02:35,033 --> 00:02:38,135
[SPEAKER_00]: And the nutriment for that is not associating with true persons.

33
00:02:40,873 --> 00:02:42,314
[SPEAKER_00]: So then, the Buddha sums it up.

34
00:02:42,354 --> 00:02:50,418
[SPEAKER_00]: It says, in this way, when the factor of not associating with true persons is fulfilled, it fulfills the factor of not listening to the true teaching, not hearing it.

35
00:02:51,259 --> 00:02:55,581
[SPEAKER_00]: When the factor of not listening to the true teaching is fulfilled, it fulfills the factor of the lack of faith.

36
00:02:57,522 --> 00:03:02,525
[SPEAKER_00]: Lack of faith factor fulfills the factor of ayoniso manasikāra.

37
00:03:03,846 --> 00:03:09,069
[SPEAKER_00]: Ayoniso manasikāra factor fulfills the factor of lack of sati sampajañña.

38
00:03:10,067 --> 00:03:11,749
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's like when it rains heavily on a mountaintop,

39
00:03:33,843 --> 00:03:36,164
[SPEAKER_00]: So, then, the rest of the sutra just goes in the reverse.

40
00:03:36,184 --> 00:03:36,485
[SPEAKER_00]: So...

41
00:03:36,505 --> 00:03:38,926
[SPEAKER_00]: Knowledge and liberation has its nutriment.

42
00:04:01,538 --> 00:04:02,898
[SPEAKER_00]: And it's not without the nutriment.

43
00:04:02,918 --> 00:04:04,659
[SPEAKER_00]: What's the nutriment for knowledge and liberation?

44
00:04:05,199 --> 00:04:06,700
[SPEAKER_00]: It's the seven awakening factors.

45
00:04:08,160 --> 00:04:11,361
[SPEAKER_00]: Seven awakening factors have the nutriment.

46
00:04:11,541 --> 00:04:13,802
[SPEAKER_00]: The nutriment is the four foundations of recollection.

47
00:04:15,363 --> 00:04:17,523
[SPEAKER_00]: The four foundations of recollection have the nutriment.

48
00:04:17,783 --> 00:04:20,224
[SPEAKER_00]: The nutriment for that is the three kinds of good conduct.

49
00:04:21,665 --> 00:04:23,605
[SPEAKER_00]: The three kinds of good conduct have the nutriment.

50
00:04:24,546 --> 00:04:28,407
[SPEAKER_00]: And the nutriment for the three kinds of good conduct is sense restraint.

51
00:04:29,706 --> 00:04:31,426
[SPEAKER_00]: Sense-restraint has its nutriment.

52
00:04:31,446 --> 00:04:32,487
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not without a nutriment.

53
00:04:33,387 --> 00:04:36,287
[SPEAKER_00]: And the nutriment for sense-restraint is sati-sampajañña.

54
00:04:38,908 --> 00:04:41,849
[SPEAKER_00]: Sati-sampajañña, recollection, awareness, have the nutriment.

55
00:04:41,929 --> 00:04:42,949
[SPEAKER_00]: And it's not without nutriment.

56
00:04:43,069 --> 00:04:48,790
[SPEAKER_00]: And the nutriment for that is attention in the light of its source, or yonisu manasikāra.

57
00:04:50,030 --> 00:04:51,691
[SPEAKER_00]: Yonisu manasikāra has its nutriment.

58
00:04:52,251 --> 00:04:53,351
[SPEAKER_00]: And that nutriment is faith.

59
00:04:53,891 --> 00:04:54,891
[SPEAKER_00]: Faith has its nutriment.

60
00:04:55,171 --> 00:04:58,092
[SPEAKER_00]: That nutriment is listening to the true, correct teachings.

61
00:05:00,446 --> 00:05:06,310
[SPEAKER_00]: Listening to the true teachings has its nutriment, and that is associating with true persons.

62
00:05:08,391 --> 00:05:11,454
[SPEAKER_00]: So, then the Buddha sums it up, and again, same.

63
00:05:11,534 --> 00:05:24,423
[SPEAKER_00]: It's like when it rains heavily on a mountaintop, when the water flows downhill to the hollow crevices, creeks become full, fill up the pools, fill up the lakes, lakes fill up the streams, streams fill up the rivers, and then the rivers become full and they fill up the ocean.

64
00:05:26,346 --> 00:05:34,013
[SPEAKER_00]: So, let's go back to the beginning and start this one by one.

65
00:05:38,877 --> 00:05:41,499
[SPEAKER_00]: So, tantha.

66
00:05:41,799 --> 00:05:42,880
[SPEAKER_00]: Start with tantha, basically.

67
00:05:43,360 --> 00:05:47,704
[SPEAKER_00]: So, there is no first point that the craving that you have in your experience

68
00:05:49,425 --> 00:05:51,526
[SPEAKER_00]: Before which it was not, to put it that way.

69
00:05:51,546 --> 00:05:54,247
[SPEAKER_00]: Before which it didn't exist, and then it just came to being.

70
00:05:54,307 --> 00:05:57,328
[SPEAKER_00]: Just appeared out of nowhere, and now it's there.

71
00:05:58,228 --> 00:06:06,872
[SPEAKER_00]: So, in other words, it's the infinite history of relationship of you and your Tantra.

72
00:06:07,112 --> 00:06:08,692
[SPEAKER_00]: It has no discernible beginning.

73
00:06:09,933 --> 00:06:10,153
[SPEAKER_00]: So,

74
00:06:11,175 --> 00:06:27,760
[SPEAKER_00]: That already indicates that this dependence that the Buddha then goes... these nutriments that he goes to explain are not sequential things, are not things that are set up in a linear way, whereby the first thing comes to be, that then results in the second thing, that then results in the third thing.

75
00:06:28,380 --> 00:06:32,481
[SPEAKER_00]: So the relationship of its dependence is not linear.

76
00:06:33,321 --> 00:06:36,582
[SPEAKER_00]: Because if it were, then the first point of craving would be manifested.

77
00:06:38,436 --> 00:06:43,377
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's already something that people would just probably quickly kind of glance over.

78
00:06:44,017 --> 00:06:46,438
[SPEAKER_00]: It says that no first point of craving is evident.

79
00:06:47,378 --> 00:06:51,359
[SPEAKER_00]: And there are other suttas that talk... refer to the same... say the same thing about avijjā.

80
00:06:51,399 --> 00:06:53,860
[SPEAKER_00]: There's no first point of avijjā that's being manifested.

81
00:06:53,920 --> 00:06:57,121
[SPEAKER_00]: Yet, avijjā is not without its cause.

82
00:06:57,961 --> 00:07:04,643
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's the whole, obviously, as we spoke in other talks, the whole principle of bodhisattva, the idea of causality.

83
00:07:06,077 --> 00:07:16,727
[SPEAKER_00]: People need to wrap their head around and understand that it's not cause and effect in terms of linear action-reaction, but it's that simultaneous dependence.

84
00:07:18,068 --> 00:07:24,194
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's like the heat of the flame is dependent on the actual wick burning.

85
00:07:26,035 --> 00:07:28,658
[SPEAKER_00]: So, those are two separate phenomena that you can discern.

86
00:07:30,448 --> 00:07:32,689
[SPEAKER_00]: But, they're not inseparable.

87
00:07:33,009 --> 00:07:38,614
[SPEAKER_00]: You cannot have the heat without flame, or have a flame that doesn't have the heat, fire that doesn't burn.

88
00:07:39,394 --> 00:07:42,697
[SPEAKER_00]: So, this is, this is, this is kind of mirrors the same relationship.

89
00:07:42,757 --> 00:07:50,342
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if you were to look for the origin before which there was no craving, to see how craving came to be, you're never going to find it.

90
00:07:51,263 --> 00:07:57,408
[SPEAKER_00]: Because that's not how things work in terms of the, the, the phenomena of craving and paticca upādāna.

91
00:07:58,362 --> 00:08:08,105
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you need to look for the nutriment that is currently responsible for maintenance of that infinite... of that craving with its infinite history.

92
00:08:09,626 --> 00:08:10,326
[SPEAKER_00]: To put it that way.

93
00:08:11,387 --> 00:08:20,590
[SPEAKER_00]: And that currently maintained nutriment, that currently maintains the infinite history of craving, is ignorance.

94
00:08:20,930 --> 00:08:21,470
[SPEAKER_00]: It's a vijā.

95
00:08:22,691 --> 00:08:26,472
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you might recognize craving in your experience,

96
00:08:28,518 --> 00:08:39,124
[SPEAKER_00]: In regard to this feeling, or in regard to that feeling, obviously, you won't be fully understanding it, but you might be able to, kind of, wrap your head around, let's say.

97
00:08:39,144 --> 00:08:41,205
[SPEAKER_00]: Let's put it that way.

98
00:08:42,706 --> 00:08:56,253
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the whole point, then, is if you're vague understanding of the phenomenon of craving, you need to look for what does that depend on.

99
00:08:57,520 --> 00:09:04,543
[SPEAKER_00]: So, not in a psychological manner, not like, hmm, what was before this thing that I'm aware of now.

100
00:09:04,683 --> 00:09:05,704
[SPEAKER_00]: There is no before.

101
00:09:08,325 --> 00:09:11,666
[SPEAKER_00]: It's more like, what is with this thing that I'm aware of now?

102
00:09:11,746 --> 00:09:17,089
[SPEAKER_00]: This craving, this ambiguous sense of craving and pressure, whatever I define it, what is with it?

103
00:09:18,069 --> 00:09:19,290
[SPEAKER_00]: What is its nutriment?

104
00:09:19,590 --> 00:09:21,131
[SPEAKER_00]: What is its necessary basis?

105
00:09:21,391 --> 00:09:22,371
[SPEAKER_00]: What is its cause?

106
00:09:23,792 --> 00:09:25,933
[SPEAKER_00]: Like simultaneously enduring present cause.

107
00:09:27,678 --> 00:09:28,799
[SPEAKER_00]: Obviously, the Buddha gives you the answer.

108
00:09:28,839 --> 00:09:29,359
[SPEAKER_00]: It's avijjā.

109
00:09:29,720 --> 00:09:36,105
[SPEAKER_00]: But now, if you question it rightly, you at least get some idea as to where to look for that avijjā.

110
00:09:36,645 --> 00:09:38,427
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's not the avijjā from the past.

111
00:09:39,845 --> 00:09:48,974
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not the avijjā from the, you know, six years ago or something, or some hidden lack of knowledge that you haven't learned yet.

112
00:09:49,515 --> 00:09:49,675
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

113
00:09:49,795 --> 00:09:58,764
[SPEAKER_00]: It's currently enduring phenomena that maintains that it's responsible for the maintenance of another currently, presently enduring phenomena of craving.

114
00:09:59,705 --> 00:10:01,547
[SPEAKER_00]: So then, that avijjā

115
00:10:03,190 --> 00:10:03,810
[SPEAKER_00]: What is it then?

116
00:10:04,111 --> 00:10:12,175
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if it is there, if it has to be there simultaneously present with the craving, because no first point of craving is evident, yet it depends on something.

117
00:10:13,836 --> 00:10:19,719
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if it depends on something, that thing also has no first point of its origin evident either.

118
00:10:20,960 --> 00:10:24,201
[SPEAKER_00]: And, as I said, Adi Sutta actually said that directly about avijjā.

119
00:10:25,022 --> 00:10:26,302
[SPEAKER_00]: So, what is that avijjā then?

120
00:10:26,983 --> 00:10:30,465
[SPEAKER_00]: When you have the phenomenon of craving, in your experience, resisting

121
00:10:31,898 --> 00:10:38,500
[SPEAKER_00]: Craving for more pleasure, resisting the discomfort, leaning towards indolence when the feeling is neutral.

122
00:10:39,720 --> 00:10:43,541
[SPEAKER_00]: What is the common theme of those three attitudes?

123
00:10:45,241 --> 00:10:48,102
[SPEAKER_00]: What is a common thread to all those three attitudes?

124
00:10:49,662 --> 00:10:50,243
[SPEAKER_00]: It's ignorance.

125
00:10:50,583 --> 00:10:56,184
[SPEAKER_00]: But what is that ignorance as a... as a... like, practically speaking, how does that manifest in your experience?

126
00:10:59,583 --> 00:11:02,824
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's already a line for reflection.

127
00:11:03,865 --> 00:11:08,406
[SPEAKER_00]: Ignorance, avijja, non-knowledge of what, ignoring, what, how.

128
00:11:08,646 --> 00:11:12,648
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not really like the lack of information, as I said.

129
00:11:13,388 --> 00:11:21,211
[SPEAKER_00]: Because then, if you were to get the information, if ignorance were truly to be information, lack of information, if you once you learned information, that moment

130
00:11:22,189 --> 00:11:30,171
[SPEAKER_00]: From then onwards, craving would disappear, because you would have removed its necessary, presently, simultaneously enduring cause.

131
00:11:34,393 --> 00:11:35,213
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's not that.

132
00:11:36,073 --> 00:11:36,814
[SPEAKER_00]: So, what is it then?

133
00:11:37,014 --> 00:11:38,474
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, I explained that in other talks.

134
00:11:39,494 --> 00:11:44,076
[SPEAKER_00]: It's the attitude of ignoring, turning a blind eye, covering up,

135
00:11:45,064 --> 00:12:03,109
[SPEAKER_00]: Exemplified through the behaviors of avoiding responsibility for your actions, for your choices, placing the origin of the pressure of lust, or ill-will, or other pressures on the world, on the things, on the objects of your attention, and so on.

136
00:12:04,169 --> 00:12:06,310
[SPEAKER_00]: So, all of those attitudes maintain ignorance.

137
00:12:07,530 --> 00:12:09,811
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's pretty much exactly what the next line says.

138
00:12:09,891 --> 00:12:12,872
[SPEAKER_00]: See, you start looking for ignorance, you see it's a certain attitude of

139
00:12:13,917 --> 00:12:19,980
[SPEAKER_00]: Being unable to help yourself, but ignore, but actively turn a blind eye.

140
00:12:21,461 --> 00:12:25,764
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, it's a valid line of reasoning to then ask a further question.

141
00:12:26,084 --> 00:12:27,084
[SPEAKER_00]: Why is it so?

142
00:12:27,725 --> 00:12:29,366
[SPEAKER_00]: Why is it that I can't help myself?

143
00:12:30,446 --> 00:12:34,509
[SPEAKER_00]: Ignore, or not want to deal with this, or just turn away, or not see through things.

144
00:12:35,209 --> 00:12:38,391
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, it's because of the five hindrances.

145
00:12:38,431 --> 00:12:41,593
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's because of those five pressures of sensuality,

146
00:12:42,459 --> 00:12:50,083
[SPEAKER_00]: Ill-will, laziness, indolence, restlessness, and then doubt.

147
00:12:52,225 --> 00:12:59,889
[SPEAKER_00]: But now, you're gonna ask yourself, okay, so when I have a pressure of doubt, or a pressure of ill-will, what is my responsibility there?

148
00:13:01,870 --> 00:13:08,294
[SPEAKER_00]: I mean, those things have arisen on their own, sure, but what is... do I... do I have some input?

149
00:13:08,554 --> 00:13:09,535
[SPEAKER_00]: Do I have some say in it?

150
00:13:09,675 --> 00:13:10,175
[SPEAKER_00]: What do I do?

151
00:13:12,170 --> 00:13:16,831
[SPEAKER_00]: And again, we address that in other talks, but that's basically when you realize, oh, you are responsible.

152
00:13:17,472 --> 00:13:29,095
[SPEAKER_00]: You're not responsible for five hindrances coming up, pressuring you, but you're responsible for taking them up, for not seeing through that pressure and taking it at face value.

153
00:13:30,075 --> 00:13:36,517
[SPEAKER_00]: So, when pressure of sensuality, a hindrance of sensuality arises, do you see through it?

154
00:13:37,643 --> 00:13:47,110
[SPEAKER_00]: As a bait that you are not picking up or do you just find yourself already, you know, full-on choking on the bait, already eating it and taking more?

155
00:13:47,150 --> 00:13:47,811
[SPEAKER_00]: Same with your will.

156
00:13:48,151 --> 00:13:48,791
[SPEAKER_00]: Same with any other.

157
00:13:48,912 --> 00:13:50,132
[SPEAKER_00]: Are you already acting out?

158
00:13:52,354 --> 00:13:53,175
[SPEAKER_00]: That is your input.

159
00:13:53,495 --> 00:14:00,060
[SPEAKER_00]: Are you acting out of those pressures or you may... or you remain there not acting out and not losing perspective of them?

160
00:14:01,630 --> 00:14:03,671
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's exactly what the next paragraph says.

161
00:14:04,251 --> 00:14:06,673
[SPEAKER_00]: The nutriment for the five hindrances is acting out.

162
00:14:07,273 --> 00:14:09,074
[SPEAKER_00]: The three kinds of misconduct.

163
00:14:09,794 --> 00:14:14,537
[SPEAKER_00]: It's basically acting out on account of these five pressures by body, speech and mind.

164
00:14:15,837 --> 00:14:22,381
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the fact that it's rooted in those five hindrances, in those five pressures, that's what makes it a misconduct.

165
00:14:23,275 --> 00:14:39,539
[SPEAKER_00]: Doesn't matter if... even if the whole world approves of what you did, of what your conduct was, on account of this pressure or that pressure, the fact that it was rooted in the five hindrances, and you took them up at a face value, and you acted out of them, it constitutes a misconduct already.

166
00:14:39,839 --> 00:14:41,880
[SPEAKER_00]: That's the reference point.

167
00:14:41,900 --> 00:14:47,101
[SPEAKER_00]: Not the world, not the tradition, not the... the peer pressure, not the culture.

168
00:14:47,301 --> 00:14:49,622
[SPEAKER_00]: It's whether it's rooted in the five hindrances.

169
00:14:51,020 --> 00:14:53,521
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if it is, it's a misconduct.

170
00:14:54,922 --> 00:15:01,766
[SPEAKER_00]: So, see how you would have started from, oh, craving, you know, phenomena that I am sort of...

171
00:15:01,786 --> 00:15:10,270
[SPEAKER_00]: I can be aware of, but it's somewhat abstract and it's certainly not something I can just... just voluntarily put a stop to.

172
00:15:10,431 --> 00:15:14,673
[SPEAKER_00]: Let's just... just switch it off or something, like a... like a... like a switch or unplug it.

173
00:15:15,793 --> 00:15:38,571
[SPEAKER_00]: But then, as you start reflecting through, you see that it depends on a certain attitude of ignoring, turning a blind eye, avoiding... avoiding transparency, basically, just kind of feeling uncomfortable with it, looking past it, distracting yourself from it, through actions that are pressuring you towards distraction, towards lust, towards aversion, towards these things.

174
00:15:39,151 --> 00:15:45,716
[SPEAKER_00]: But now, from that kind of semi-abstract, ambiguous, you came down to three kinds of misconduct.

175
00:15:46,857 --> 00:15:51,301
[SPEAKER_00]: In other words, choices that you choose to act out of.

176
00:15:53,262 --> 00:15:56,305
[SPEAKER_00]: So, there is now no more abstraction there.

177
00:15:56,525 --> 00:15:57,926
[SPEAKER_00]: I mean, you can theorize all you want.

178
00:15:59,023 --> 00:16:04,206
[SPEAKER_00]: But the bottom line is, whenever you make a choice to act, you choose to do so, and you know it.

179
00:16:04,927 --> 00:16:18,335
[SPEAKER_00]: You can justify it, obviously, retroactively, and say, oh, it was because of this, or because of that, but when you were making it, you knew it's because you were making it, and because you were pressured to do it, and you wanted to act out of the pressure, and you wanted to get rid of this comfort, and you acted out.

180
00:16:18,875 --> 00:16:22,317
[SPEAKER_00]: Everything else, then, any psychologization that comes afterwards, it's second to it.

181
00:16:23,613 --> 00:16:33,718
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the five hindrances directly depend on acting out of those five pressures that try to hinder you through acting by body, speech and mind.

182
00:16:34,878 --> 00:16:37,580
[SPEAKER_00]: So, those are the actual nutriments for the five hindrances.

183
00:16:40,021 --> 00:16:41,582
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, it goes to say...

184
00:16:41,642 --> 00:16:49,825
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the next line of reflection, again, I mean, we follow the suttas, but obviously, you can just ask yourself these things and look for these phenomena in your experience.

185
00:16:49,926 --> 00:16:52,927
[SPEAKER_00]: So, three kinds of bodily misconduct.

186
00:16:53,927 --> 00:17:02,611
[SPEAKER_00]: So, whenever you act out of greed, aversion, distraction, by body, by speech, by mind, and you don't even have to go into, like, two subtle layers of, you know, what's this?

187
00:17:03,131 --> 00:17:03,932
[SPEAKER_00]: Where was this rooted?

188
00:17:03,952 --> 00:17:05,132
[SPEAKER_00]: What was my intention behind this?

189
00:17:05,633 --> 00:17:06,573
[SPEAKER_00]: Take the obvious stuff.

190
00:17:07,333 --> 00:17:09,534
[SPEAKER_00]: Sensuality, ill-will, doubt.

191
00:17:10,235 --> 00:17:11,255
[SPEAKER_00]: Just take the obvious stuff.

192
00:17:12,576 --> 00:17:20,339
[SPEAKER_00]: And see, ask yourself, acting out of the obvious stuff, what is the necessary basis for that?

193
00:17:20,439 --> 00:17:21,400
[SPEAKER_00]: What is the currently

194
00:17:24,025 --> 00:17:31,889
[SPEAKER_00]: Simultaneously enduring present cause for me to keep acting out of these things.

195
00:17:36,811 --> 00:17:43,214
[SPEAKER_00]: Why do I... why do I keep, like, going towards sensuality or going towards ill-will and so on?

196
00:17:43,994 --> 00:17:45,595
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, because the pressure got too much.

197
00:17:47,096 --> 00:17:49,777
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you obviously need to train in regard to the pressure,

198
00:17:51,478 --> 00:17:58,505
[SPEAKER_00]: But you don't start from the side of already being able to kind of contain and not act out of pressure.

199
00:17:58,525 --> 00:18:01,107
[SPEAKER_00]: You start from the side of being completely overwhelmed by the pressure.

200
00:18:01,848 --> 00:18:03,689
[SPEAKER_00]: That's where virtue has to come first.

201
00:18:03,809 --> 00:18:09,955
[SPEAKER_00]: It creates some sort of container for you to at least have a certain line that you will not cross.

202
00:18:10,696 --> 00:18:11,457
[SPEAKER_00]: No amount of...

203
00:18:11,537 --> 00:18:15,100
[SPEAKER_00]: It doesn't matter what kind of pressure is put on you to act and do this.

204
00:18:17,022 --> 00:18:17,202
[SPEAKER_00]: So,

205
00:18:18,595 --> 00:18:27,043
[SPEAKER_00]: You act out of body, speech and mind when basically you cannot contain, you cannot endure rightly on the right level that pressure of the hindrances.

206
00:18:27,764 --> 00:18:30,547
[SPEAKER_00]: So then, why can you not endure rightly?

207
00:18:32,589 --> 00:18:33,449
[SPEAKER_00]: Why do you act out?

208
00:18:34,050 --> 00:18:36,312
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, because you lack sense restraint.

209
00:18:37,013 --> 00:18:42,258
[SPEAKER_00]: You were lacking foresight in regard to your own capacity

210
00:18:43,575 --> 00:18:48,546
[SPEAKER_00]: To withstand and contain and endure the pressure of hindrances without acting out.

211
00:18:49,842 --> 00:18:53,404
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you went into the unsuitable sights, sounds, smells, tastes, touches.

212
00:18:53,904 --> 00:18:55,445
[SPEAKER_00]: That's the purpose of sense restraint.

213
00:18:55,505 --> 00:18:59,987
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not saying no to everything blindly because it's bad in and of itself.

214
00:19:00,647 --> 00:19:00,787
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

215
00:19:01,207 --> 00:19:14,253
[SPEAKER_00]: Purpose of sense restraint is basically to enable you in the beginning to be able to to have some distance between you and the pressure that's trying to overwhelm you and push you towards acting out of body, speech and mind.

216
00:19:16,973 --> 00:19:20,855
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's like a... it's like a... it's like an... like an indirect effect.

217
00:19:21,255 --> 00:19:36,201
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you... you foresee, like, oh, if I go in such and such environment, it will be very likely, highly likely, that I will... that the pressure towards sensuality or real will will increase to the point of I don't know if I will be able to restrain myself.

218
00:19:37,561 --> 00:19:41,263
[SPEAKER_00]: So, then you prevent those scenarios from arising in the first place.

219
00:19:42,178 --> 00:19:46,162
[SPEAKER_00]: That's the whole point of restraining eye, ear, nose and so on.

220
00:19:46,583 --> 00:19:56,353
[SPEAKER_00]: So, whenever you practice restraint, you need to practice it with this context, with this type of informed attitude.

221
00:19:57,447 --> 00:19:59,068
[SPEAKER_00]: Because that's what gives it its purpose.

222
00:19:59,609 --> 00:20:02,551
[SPEAKER_00]: I'm not just saying no to sights and sounds and everything.

223
00:20:02,571 --> 00:20:07,054
[SPEAKER_00]: No, I'm doing it because I keep an eye on the mind that's going to be overwhelmed with the pressure.

224
00:20:07,915 --> 00:20:12,838
[SPEAKER_00]: And if I'm not sure to what extent, well, to that extent, I need to be careful how much I expose it.

225
00:20:14,719 --> 00:20:16,601
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's why there's a lack of the sense restraint.

226
00:20:17,481 --> 00:20:22,285
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you're basically having an active attitude of non-sense restraint.

227
00:20:23,622 --> 00:20:28,164
[SPEAKER_00]: It's the simultaneously present nutriment for the three kinds of misconduct.

228
00:20:28,244 --> 00:20:32,425
[SPEAKER_00]: When that attitude builds up, you will act out.

229
00:20:32,445 --> 00:20:34,006
[SPEAKER_00]: Because that's where you're heading, basically.

230
00:20:35,646 --> 00:20:39,848
[SPEAKER_00]: So, what is then the cause for the lack of sense restraint?

231
00:20:39,948 --> 00:20:41,008
[SPEAKER_00]: What is its nutriment?

232
00:20:41,368 --> 00:20:42,769
[SPEAKER_00]: Simultaneously present nutriment?

233
00:20:43,809 --> 00:20:45,409
[SPEAKER_00]: It's the lack of sati, sampajañña.

234
00:20:45,429 --> 00:20:49,591
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's the lack of non-forgetfulness and awareness.

235
00:20:50,478 --> 00:21:04,403
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's basically, it's lacking the recognition of here and now, of the importance of preventing certain circumstances, that would then result in pressures building up and overwhelming your mind.

236
00:21:04,883 --> 00:21:05,823
[SPEAKER_00]: And not forgetting that.

237
00:21:06,963 --> 00:21:12,685
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, you might feel, oh, I feel pretty good today, my mind feels stable, I can probably handle it and so on.

238
00:21:13,306 --> 00:21:14,066
[SPEAKER_00]: But that's not the point.

239
00:21:14,206 --> 00:21:17,367
[SPEAKER_00]: You remember then, that such environment

240
00:21:18,912 --> 00:21:23,035
[SPEAKER_00]: Or a certain environments are always unbeneficial, are always to be avoided.

241
00:21:23,655 --> 00:21:24,876
[SPEAKER_00]: And then you don't compromise that.

242
00:21:25,156 --> 00:21:27,818
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, great, if you can handle it, perfect.

243
00:21:28,679 --> 00:21:30,960
[SPEAKER_00]: But it's even better if there is nothing to handle to begin with.

244
00:21:32,822 --> 00:21:42,629
[SPEAKER_00]: And, eh... on top of it all, sooner or later, things will come your way that no amount of sense restraint will be able to, eh... prevent the pressure from coming up.

245
00:21:43,496 --> 00:21:59,103
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's why you practice before those sickness, aging, or greater misfortunes that come and overwhelm your mind, through... exactly, through sense restraint, so to be able to contain the pressure, and then learning how to not act out of your own pressure, of your own citta.

246
00:21:59,783 --> 00:22:07,306
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's why lacking the attitude of that right memory, like literally, like remembering the right things, the things that are important to you,

247
00:22:08,347 --> 00:22:08,947
[SPEAKER_00]: You forget.

248
00:22:09,728 --> 00:22:10,768
[SPEAKER_00]: You stop thinking about it.

249
00:22:11,769 --> 00:22:21,535
[SPEAKER_00]: Sure, if somebody were to ask you, you would remember, but you're not actively having it there as a kind of framework of your actions and choices throughout the day.

250
00:22:21,635 --> 00:22:25,137
[SPEAKER_00]: What you're going to look at, see, taste, smell, and so on.

251
00:22:25,177 --> 00:22:28,099
[SPEAKER_00]: Where you're going to go, who you're going to talk to, who you're going to meet, and so on.

252
00:22:28,860 --> 00:22:32,562
[SPEAKER_00]: But if you do not forget what's important,

253
00:22:34,242 --> 00:22:37,765
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, those choices, when they present themselves, would be put in the right light.

254
00:22:38,246 --> 00:22:44,811
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, I could go over there and see such and such person, but last time I was there, I got really wound up.

255
00:22:45,831 --> 00:22:49,433
[SPEAKER_00]: And my mind overwhelms and I said stuff I shouldn't and so on.

256
00:22:49,513 --> 00:22:51,374
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's like, oh, I'd better restrain myself.

257
00:22:51,494 --> 00:22:52,535
[SPEAKER_00]: Same.

258
00:22:52,595 --> 00:22:59,919
[SPEAKER_00]: Or go to, like, I don't know, parties with alcohol and stuff, where priests will be compromised, which will then result in compromising everything else.

259
00:22:59,979 --> 00:23:03,962
[SPEAKER_00]: That all comes out of, basically, lacking recollection and awareness correctly.

260
00:23:04,282 --> 00:23:10,325
[SPEAKER_00]: Lacking the remembering of what's important to you.

261
00:23:10,645 --> 00:23:13,227
[SPEAKER_00]: And then it says, so what is the reason for lacking that?

262
00:23:13,267 --> 00:23:14,928
[SPEAKER_00]: What is the reason for you forgetting stuff?

263
00:23:16,678 --> 00:23:19,219
[SPEAKER_00]: And the reason is ayoniso manasikāra.

264
00:23:19,979 --> 00:23:22,100
[SPEAKER_00]: So, attention not in the light of its source.

265
00:23:22,580 --> 00:23:29,102
[SPEAKER_00]: So, in other words, the reason for that is that when you do... because your attention always has to rest on things, on stuff.

266
00:23:30,062 --> 00:23:31,402
[SPEAKER_00]: Sight, sound, smell, taste, touches, thoughts.

267
00:23:33,083 --> 00:23:39,345
[SPEAKER_00]: But that doesn't mean that you need to ignore the background of it, the context.

268
00:23:40,725 --> 00:23:43,126
[SPEAKER_00]: So, whenever your attention is resting on something,

269
00:23:44,007 --> 00:23:52,899
[SPEAKER_00]: At the back of your mind, it's going to be already a form of subtle, implicit reason for resting on those things.

270
00:23:54,341 --> 00:23:58,927
[SPEAKER_00]: That reason can be neutral, but it can be unwholesome.

271
00:24:00,966 --> 00:24:10,290
[SPEAKER_00]: It can be wholesome, unwholesome and neutral, in a sense that you could have a good reason to be attending to this, you could have a bad reason to be attending to this, i.e.

272
00:24:10,330 --> 00:24:12,571
[SPEAKER_00]: greed, aversion, delusion, non-greed, non-aversion, non-delusion.

273
00:24:13,051 --> 00:24:15,132
[SPEAKER_00]: Or you could have a neutral reason for attending to this.

274
00:24:15,232 --> 00:24:26,097
[SPEAKER_00]: But the point is, either of those reasons, if you are not discerning the reason for your attention resting on that, simultaneous to your attention resting on those things,

275
00:24:27,159 --> 00:24:30,220
[SPEAKER_00]: You are in the state of ayoniso, manasikāra.

276
00:24:30,740 --> 00:24:31,680
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's the point here.

277
00:24:33,180 --> 00:24:35,201
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's not just, I need to have a good reason.

278
00:24:35,441 --> 00:24:36,881
[SPEAKER_00]: You need to know the reason.

279
00:24:39,622 --> 00:24:55,425
[SPEAKER_00]: So, whenever you find your mind in a certain state, or find your thoughts occupied with certain things, instead of trying to resolve and manage the feeling that came with it, whether it's pleasant, unpleasant or neutral, you want to see the backdrop of it.

280
00:24:56,650 --> 00:25:03,995
[SPEAKER_00]: In other words, what is the reason for my attention revolving around this or resting on these things?

281
00:25:04,015 --> 00:25:06,777
[SPEAKER_00]: Not change it, not get focused on something else.

282
00:25:07,417 --> 00:25:10,399
[SPEAKER_00]: Either way, the reason of your focusing will be unknown.

283
00:25:10,799 --> 00:25:11,940
[SPEAKER_00]: It will be ayoniso.

284
00:25:13,861 --> 00:25:17,164
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if you find yourself basically

285
00:25:18,246 --> 00:25:23,188
[SPEAKER_00]: Not wanting to think about it, or not wanting to drill these things, like, why am I doing this?

286
00:25:23,248 --> 00:25:23,908
[SPEAKER_00]: Why am I thinking this?

287
00:25:23,948 --> 00:25:25,328
[SPEAKER_00]: Why is my attention resting on this?

288
00:25:25,709 --> 00:25:34,071
[SPEAKER_00]: Because it's kind of bothersome for sure, cumbersome, unpleasant, like...

289
00:25:34,191 --> 00:25:36,672
[SPEAKER_00]: It's just not something that you would naturally want to do.

290
00:25:38,753 --> 00:25:40,454
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's why you're kind of avoiding doing it.

291
00:25:41,154 --> 00:25:44,015
[SPEAKER_00]: And if you ask yourself, well, okay, now say you learn,

292
00:25:44,738 --> 00:25:46,019
[SPEAKER_00]: That, no, you need to do this.

293
00:25:46,799 --> 00:25:54,565
[SPEAKER_00]: Whenever you find your attention resting on something or planning something, you need to know on the basis of what is this happening.

294
00:25:56,186 --> 00:26:14,259
[SPEAKER_00]: And you're always going to boil down to, basically, as I said before, it's going to be either on the basis of pleasant feeling, you're leaning towards wanting it more, the basis of unpleasant feeling, you're kind of resisting it, or the basis of, like, a neutral feeling that's already causing a certain kind of unease and you want to be oblivious to it.

295
00:26:15,443 --> 00:26:20,790
[SPEAKER_00]: It doesn't go into some, as I said, great ancient history that you need to discern where it all came to be.

296
00:26:20,810 --> 00:26:21,391
[SPEAKER_00]: It's right there.

297
00:26:21,612 --> 00:26:23,394
[SPEAKER_00]: All of these things are simultaneously there.

298
00:26:23,494 --> 00:26:25,036
[SPEAKER_00]: Just layers of the same principle.

299
00:26:26,498 --> 00:26:32,827
[SPEAKER_00]: So, why are you then not always aware of the background?

300
00:26:35,493 --> 00:26:36,533
[SPEAKER_00]: In that indirect sense.

301
00:26:36,894 --> 00:26:42,596
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, you know, I can be attending to this or to that, but I can at the same time know why am I doing that.

302
00:26:43,156 --> 00:26:44,496
[SPEAKER_00]: I can see where it's rooted.

303
00:26:44,596 --> 00:26:49,518
[SPEAKER_00]: I can see whether it's kind of wholesome, whether it's unwholesome, whether it's neutral.

304
00:26:49,798 --> 00:26:50,778
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's the important part.

305
00:26:50,818 --> 00:26:51,359
[SPEAKER_00]: To see it.

306
00:26:52,639 --> 00:26:54,080
[SPEAKER_00]: So, why are you not seeing it?

307
00:26:54,580 --> 00:26:56,620
[SPEAKER_00]: It's because you haven't drilled it deep enough.

308
00:26:57,281 --> 00:26:58,521
[SPEAKER_00]: You haven't practiced it.

309
00:26:58,561 --> 00:27:03,543
[SPEAKER_00]: You haven't made the effort to actually look that way sufficiently enough.

310
00:27:05,400 --> 00:27:05,960
[SPEAKER_00]: There is not.

311
00:27:05,980 --> 00:27:08,201
[SPEAKER_00]: It's like you haven't trained yourself, basically.

312
00:27:09,681 --> 00:27:11,061
[SPEAKER_00]: So, why haven't you done that?

313
00:27:11,802 --> 00:27:13,462
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, it's because you lacked faith.

314
00:27:15,002 --> 00:27:24,945
[SPEAKER_00]: And lacking faith doesn't mean you lacked some, you know, inspiration on the level of of poetry or uplifted spirit of the Buddha's words or something.

315
00:27:24,985 --> 00:27:25,105
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

316
00:27:25,125 --> 00:27:26,165
[SPEAKER_00]: It means you lacked faith.

317
00:27:26,185 --> 00:27:33,927
[SPEAKER_00]: It means, basically, it didn't really... you refused to believe

318
00:27:35,272 --> 00:27:39,215
[SPEAKER_00]: How important that is, to practice this yoniso.

319
00:27:40,416 --> 00:27:41,737
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, you lacked faith, basically.

320
00:27:41,757 --> 00:27:46,341
[SPEAKER_00]: It means you lacked the belief in importance of application of it.

321
00:27:46,661 --> 00:27:47,282
[SPEAKER_00]: That's what it means.

322
00:27:48,843 --> 00:27:49,424
[SPEAKER_00]: You dismissed it.

323
00:27:50,645 --> 00:27:56,029
[SPEAKER_00]: You heard it, but you thought, well, yeah, sure, that could be one aspect of it, but I prefer this aspect.

324
00:27:56,109 --> 00:27:58,131
[SPEAKER_00]: And then you kind of concocted your own thing.

325
00:27:58,291 --> 00:27:59,532
[SPEAKER_00]: And you haven't done the drilling.

326
00:28:03,836 --> 00:28:04,036
[SPEAKER_00]: So,

327
00:28:05,477 --> 00:28:06,998
[SPEAKER_00]: Why would then a person lack faith?

328
00:28:08,159 --> 00:28:13,684
[SPEAKER_00]: Why would then a person not drill in the importance of this principle?

329
00:28:13,944 --> 00:28:16,367
[SPEAKER_00]: Why would it kind of brush it off?

330
00:28:16,427 --> 00:28:18,088
[SPEAKER_00]: Or dismiss it?

331
00:28:18,248 --> 00:28:19,409
[SPEAKER_00]: Or change it into something else?

332
00:28:20,790 --> 00:28:22,852
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, because they haven't learned how important it is.

333
00:28:23,693 --> 00:28:25,955
[SPEAKER_00]: In other words, they haven't listened to the actual dhamma.

334
00:28:27,210 --> 00:28:31,512
[SPEAKER_00]: They might have been hearing things about the dhamma, but that's not necessarily the true teaching.

335
00:28:32,132 --> 00:28:37,554
[SPEAKER_00]: So, not everybody who speaks the dhamma, even if they just parrot the Buddha's words, is speaking the true teaching.

336
00:28:39,054 --> 00:28:42,076
[SPEAKER_00]: The true teaching is the teaching that emphasizes things that should be emphasized.

337
00:28:43,716 --> 00:28:48,958
[SPEAKER_00]: Not just parrots what somebody else says, and then leads you to figure it out, or make your own way, whatever feels right to you.

338
00:28:50,078 --> 00:28:56,661
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if a person lacks faith to sustain the application of constant questioning of

339
00:28:58,395 --> 00:28:59,655
[SPEAKER_00]: Why is my attention resting on this?

340
00:28:59,735 --> 00:29:01,116
[SPEAKER_00]: What's the back backdrop?

341
00:29:01,176 --> 00:29:03,136
[SPEAKER_00]: What's the back state of my mind?

342
00:29:03,556 --> 00:29:05,637
[SPEAKER_00]: Where is my mind leaning?

343
00:29:06,917 --> 00:29:11,758
[SPEAKER_00]: It's because they haven't, as I said, accepted the fact that it's really important.

344
00:29:11,798 --> 00:29:13,979
[SPEAKER_00]: They don't believe it's important to do it.

345
00:29:14,739 --> 00:29:24,341
[SPEAKER_00]: And they don't believe it's important to do it because they haven't really heard or listened or understood enough of why it is important to do it.

346
00:29:25,584 --> 00:29:33,107
[SPEAKER_00]: So, they haven't... or they never heard somebody emphasizes that as really important thing and that's why they never really paid much attention to it.

347
00:29:33,647 --> 00:29:35,448
[SPEAKER_00]: Or they might have heard it and then they dismissed it.

348
00:29:36,948 --> 00:29:41,450
[SPEAKER_00]: So, why would they then not have listened to the true teaching?

349
00:29:42,930 --> 00:29:46,492
[SPEAKER_00]: And the nutriment is because they were not associating with true persons.

350
00:29:46,512 --> 00:29:47,552
[SPEAKER_00]: In other words, with right people.

351
00:29:47,632 --> 00:29:49,313
[SPEAKER_00]: With people that can actually teach them this.

352
00:29:50,073 --> 00:29:54,375
[SPEAKER_00]: And being associating with them doesn't mean socializing and just sitting in a company and

353
00:29:55,654 --> 00:30:00,636
[SPEAKER_00]: Soaking up their spiritual energy, you know, means hearing this from somebody who understood it.

354
00:30:02,837 --> 00:30:16,963
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you want to learn a craft, you need to go to somebody who knows the craft, who actually can teach you and can explain in their own words the principles behind it, not just sort of recite the government curriculum or something.

355
00:30:18,024 --> 00:30:20,185
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's what it means not associating with true persons.

356
00:30:21,005 --> 00:30:22,246
[SPEAKER_00]: Now, you can ask yourself, well,

357
00:30:23,387 --> 00:30:25,568
[SPEAKER_00]: Why have I not been associating with true people?

358
00:30:26,408 --> 00:30:27,769
[SPEAKER_00]: With people that know the dhamma?

359
00:30:28,929 --> 00:30:34,651
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's where things get, obviously, like, you start questioning yourself and they can be, well, maybe because you never met one.

360
00:30:34,671 --> 00:30:37,292
[SPEAKER_00]: Maybe, maybe you might have met them and dismissed them.

361
00:30:38,532 --> 00:30:42,034
[SPEAKER_00]: Maybe you didn't like their energy or something and you didn't want to listen what they have to say.

362
00:30:43,354 --> 00:30:47,135
[SPEAKER_00]: Maybe you liked everything, but you haven't actually paid attention to what's being said.

363
00:30:47,496 --> 00:30:51,597
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, so even people that you associated yourself with, in terms of learning the dhamma,

364
00:30:53,138 --> 00:31:05,650
[SPEAKER_00]: It's like, have I really unequivocally understood for myself what they emphasize and seen that as beneficial or not as beneficial?

365
00:31:06,431 --> 00:31:10,675
[SPEAKER_00]: So, in other words, you can't just sit and listen to the dhamma even if it's the Buddha himself talking.

366
00:31:10,715 --> 00:31:12,537
[SPEAKER_00]: You need to make the effort to understand it.

367
00:31:13,665 --> 00:31:27,333
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's that other sutta that says the factor for the rising on the right view is utterance of another, of somebody who understood it, who knows how to teach you, which would be the equivalent of associating with the true person.

368
00:31:27,693 --> 00:31:31,115
[SPEAKER_00]: But then the other factor is that yoniso manasikāra.

369
00:31:32,136 --> 00:31:40,781
[SPEAKER_00]: So it's already being able to have the context on your reactions to what you hear, and not just take them at face value.

370
00:31:40,821 --> 00:31:42,082
[SPEAKER_00]: I like this, I don't like this.

371
00:31:44,282 --> 00:31:53,446
[SPEAKER_00]: One of the reasons that the Buddha said that a person wouldn't get the right view, even if they are being taught by the Buddha himself, is they have the attitude of finding faults.

372
00:31:53,987 --> 00:31:54,967
[SPEAKER_00]: So they're looking for faults.

373
00:31:55,547 --> 00:31:57,268
[SPEAKER_00]: That's already coming out of the preference.

374
00:31:57,869 --> 00:32:02,091
[SPEAKER_00]: That's not being addressed through yoniso manasikāra.

375
00:32:02,111 --> 00:32:03,211
[SPEAKER_00]: See, I have a preference.

376
00:32:03,291 --> 00:32:04,512
[SPEAKER_00]: I like things, I dislike things.

377
00:32:04,992 --> 00:32:07,053
[SPEAKER_00]: I hear this person speak, I don't like it.

378
00:32:08,883 --> 00:32:16,930
[SPEAKER_00]: But if I'm, myself, interested in my own welfare, in my own benefit, I can't take that at face value and just dismiss them because I don't like it.

379
00:32:17,631 --> 00:32:22,775
[SPEAKER_00]: Equally, I can't, at a face value, accept them because I like how they speak.

380
00:32:24,217 --> 00:32:25,338
[SPEAKER_00]: We're back on to yoniso.

381
00:32:25,778 --> 00:32:27,740
[SPEAKER_00]: Either way, I need to know.

382
00:32:29,103 --> 00:32:32,169
[SPEAKER_00]: Where is this leaning, this preference, where is it rooted?

383
00:32:32,309 --> 00:32:33,491
[SPEAKER_00]: What is my motivation?

384
00:32:33,531 --> 00:32:34,372
[SPEAKER_00]: What is my intention?

385
00:32:34,453 --> 00:32:40,463
[SPEAKER_00]: What is the reason, like, simultaneously enduring reason for my dislike or like?

386
00:32:40,784 --> 00:32:42,507
[SPEAKER_00]: And then put it in that context.

387
00:32:43,697 --> 00:32:53,545
[SPEAKER_00]: So, I really dislike what this person is saying, but I see that as a mere emotional preference, that I cannot rationally justify, if I'm honest and transparent.

388
00:32:54,345 --> 00:32:59,590
[SPEAKER_00]: So then, if you are actually clear, even with all the dislike you have, you're still going to be hearing what they say.

389
00:33:00,851 --> 00:33:05,735
[SPEAKER_00]: And you'll be able to actually think about it, and take it on, and really investigate for yourself.

390
00:33:06,515 --> 00:33:12,400
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, so he emphasizes this, and I really dislike him, I can't stand him, but what he's saying,

391
00:33:13,653 --> 00:33:16,135
[SPEAKER_00]: Reminds me of this sutta, or reminds me of that sutta.

392
00:33:16,835 --> 00:33:18,416
[SPEAKER_00]: So, maybe there is something more to it.

393
00:33:18,456 --> 00:33:19,416
[SPEAKER_00]: So, let me try and apply it.

394
00:33:19,517 --> 00:33:21,598
[SPEAKER_00]: I still hate him, but let me try and apply it.

395
00:33:21,698 --> 00:33:23,039
[SPEAKER_00]: Basically, that's how you progress.

396
00:33:23,819 --> 00:33:24,239
[SPEAKER_00]: Doesn't matter.

397
00:33:24,259 --> 00:33:25,520
[SPEAKER_00]: You put your preference aside.

398
00:33:25,600 --> 00:33:25,980
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, same.

399
00:33:26,361 --> 00:33:28,262
[SPEAKER_00]: I'm just so inspired by this person.

400
00:33:29,022 --> 00:33:32,604
[SPEAKER_00]: It's just when he speaks, like for days, I'm just just sort of floating.

401
00:33:32,744 --> 00:33:35,826
[SPEAKER_00]: Just nothing can touch me, because my mind is so uplifted.

402
00:33:35,866 --> 00:33:37,627
[SPEAKER_00]: It's like, yeah, but have you understood?

403
00:33:37,947 --> 00:33:38,628
[SPEAKER_00]: What are they saying?

404
00:33:39,674 --> 00:33:45,039
[SPEAKER_00]: Are they reciting poetic verses to you, or are they actually telling you what the Buddha was saying?

405
00:33:46,581 --> 00:33:57,772
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you might be inspired all you want, but if you start thinking about what's being emphasized, and what the practice is for me to do, you wouldn't see it if it's adding up or not adding up.

406
00:33:58,773 --> 00:34:02,276
[SPEAKER_00]: And if you're inspired by somebody, but things are not adding up,

407
00:34:03,262 --> 00:34:05,083
[SPEAKER_00]: You'll know which one to emphasize yourself.

408
00:34:05,283 --> 00:34:07,243
[SPEAKER_00]: You need to emphasize adding up of practice.

409
00:34:07,783 --> 00:34:09,364
[SPEAKER_00]: Inspiration or non is secondary.

410
00:34:10,044 --> 00:34:14,066
[SPEAKER_00]: Same if you're really annoyed by somebody, but what they're saying is really adding up.

411
00:34:14,826 --> 00:34:17,327
[SPEAKER_00]: Again, you will be able to not act out of that preference.

412
00:34:17,867 --> 00:34:25,949
[SPEAKER_00]: But for that, a person, every individual person, needs to make that effort, to like uncover that, those preferences, uncover the roots of those preferences.

413
00:34:25,969 --> 00:34:27,770
[SPEAKER_00]: That's where, that's already the beginning of yoniso.

414
00:34:28,370 --> 00:34:30,731
[SPEAKER_00]: That's all, that's that transparency that we often talk about.

415
00:34:32,020 --> 00:34:36,262
[SPEAKER_00]: So, neither of these things are just kind of circumstantial factors.

416
00:34:36,402 --> 00:34:38,263
[SPEAKER_00]: They're just one slot into the other, into the other.

417
00:34:38,684 --> 00:34:47,328
[SPEAKER_00]: All of these things are basically results of the layers upon layers upon layers of your own attitude, of your own overlooking, of your own lack of transparency, of your own acting out.

418
00:34:51,351 --> 00:34:52,131
[SPEAKER_00]: So, then, eh...

419
00:34:57,314 --> 00:34:59,475
[SPEAKER_00]: So, then, in reverse, basically,

420
00:35:01,552 --> 00:35:05,873
[SPEAKER_00]: It goes to say that knowledge and liberation is pretty much the result of the opposite.

421
00:35:05,973 --> 00:35:08,694
[SPEAKER_00]: So, full enlightenment is the result of the opposite.

422
00:35:08,794 --> 00:35:09,314
[SPEAKER_00]: Nothing else.

423
00:35:11,534 --> 00:35:13,415
[SPEAKER_00]: And I think there was a talk I did long time ago.

424
00:35:13,435 --> 00:35:16,115
[SPEAKER_00]: It's like self-honesty can make you an arahant or something like that.

425
00:35:16,655 --> 00:35:18,256
[SPEAKER_00]: Which basically talks exactly about this.

426
00:35:18,836 --> 00:35:29,318
[SPEAKER_00]: If you start with the transparency, which is already the beginning of the yoniso, and obviously, yes, if you hear somebody emphasizing things that need to be emphasized,

427
00:35:30,715 --> 00:35:36,838
[SPEAKER_00]: You won't fall into a trap dismissing it on account of preference or glorifying it on account of preference, either way not doing it.

428
00:35:37,499 --> 00:35:39,780
[SPEAKER_00]: You'll actually be doing it and trying to see it for yourself.

429
00:35:41,861 --> 00:35:50,905
[SPEAKER_00]: That transparency would then lead to basically you associating with true persons.

430
00:35:50,966 --> 00:35:59,490
[SPEAKER_00]: In other words, listening to them, learning from them more and more and more, which increases transparency and obviously increases the field of knowledge that can then help you discern things.

431
00:36:00,943 --> 00:36:05,469
[SPEAKER_00]: That would then lead to the listening to the actual true teaching, obviously.

432
00:36:05,489 --> 00:36:09,014
[SPEAKER_00]: Like being able to recognize the true teaching as well, for yourself.

433
00:36:10,375 --> 00:36:14,902
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, you wouldn't have a problem to believe that doing these

434
00:36:16,473 --> 00:36:29,176
[SPEAKER_00]: Drills and trainings of constantly unraveling and uncovering where your intention and motivation is rooted in regard to everything that you do or choose to attend, you would have faith in that.

435
00:36:29,556 --> 00:36:42,119
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, you wouldn't need to convince yourself that I need to keep training this, because you already, on account of associating, learning about the right emphasis, you already have a good grasp that that's what needs to be emphasized.

436
00:36:42,639 --> 00:36:45,880
[SPEAKER_00]: You might be careless and not do it, but you certainly would have enough

437
00:36:46,700 --> 00:36:49,220
[SPEAKER_00]: Faith and belief in actually applying it.

438
00:36:51,481 --> 00:36:56,402
[SPEAKER_00]: If you do that long enough, you would have sufficiently developed yoniso manasikāra.

439
00:36:56,482 --> 00:37:11,964
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you would have learned as a mode of being to, whenever you attend to things, you're attending them with a corner of your eye being aware of its source, knowing its basis, knowing the motivation behind your actions in regard to anything.

440
00:37:11,984 --> 00:37:15,445
[SPEAKER_00]: Doesn't matter how subtle or big or small, fast or slow.

441
00:37:16,950 --> 00:37:20,794
[SPEAKER_00]: But that can only come with constantly uncovering that, learning to question yourself.

442
00:37:23,017 --> 00:37:27,081
[SPEAKER_00]: So, transparency, listening to the right teaching,

443
00:37:27,790 --> 00:37:38,953
[SPEAKER_00]: You would obviously believe then in the application of it, and then you would be, through doing it, you would be developing that very yoniso manasikāra, which then means you would be recollected and aware.

444
00:37:39,293 --> 00:37:42,074
[SPEAKER_00]: You would not be forgetful of things that are important to you.

445
00:37:42,954 --> 00:37:54,177
[SPEAKER_00]: Because even if the things are way too precious, way too busy, too many moving parts, you can't follow every single instance of it, because it's just too many things happening, the broad picture remains visible.

446
00:37:55,097 --> 00:37:56,938
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, you know things that you wouldn't do.

447
00:37:57,926 --> 00:38:03,010
[SPEAKER_00]: You know things that should be de-emphasized and not engaged with and you don't forget that.

448
00:38:03,671 --> 00:38:16,381
[SPEAKER_00]: Simply through not forgetting that, the mind cannot overwhelm you or make you make choices that would then take you in the unsuitable environments that would then result in increase of the pressure that you cannot possibly control and then you would act out.

449
00:38:17,922 --> 00:38:24,928
[SPEAKER_00]: So, transparency, the same thread, would then result in fulfillment of the sense restraint.

450
00:38:25,820 --> 00:38:34,205
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the same thread that then maintains knowing what's important, is then the thread that makes you actually exercise that.

451
00:38:34,585 --> 00:38:43,310
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, because this passion is important to me, I won't go to a crazy music festival with alcohol and drugs and everything else.

452
00:38:44,822 --> 00:38:50,326
[SPEAKER_00]: Obviously, I'm giving the course examples, but the same principle can then be applied to lots of other things, where you see a direct correlation.

453
00:38:50,726 --> 00:38:52,507
[SPEAKER_00]: If I go there, this is what's going to result.

454
00:38:52,667 --> 00:39:02,994
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, even if somehow, magically, I manage to restrain myself, in terms of not act out, I've already lost the restraint by choosing to go in that environment in the first place.

455
00:39:03,014 --> 00:39:04,114
[SPEAKER_00]: Because it's completely unnecessary.

456
00:39:05,935 --> 00:39:11,159
[SPEAKER_00]: So then, if you are fulfilling sense restraint, if you never compromise on the level of your choice,

457
00:39:12,395 --> 00:39:16,619
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, sure, if you end up in this environment completely out of your hand, that's a different story.

458
00:39:17,920 --> 00:39:24,146
[SPEAKER_00]: But the the the the the factor there is whether you chose it, whether you chose to give in to those things on that level.

459
00:39:24,326 --> 00:39:26,708
[SPEAKER_00]: If you did, well, that's what we just discussed.

460
00:39:26,828 --> 00:39:32,674
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why you would not be able to sustain mindfulness and not acting out of pressure.

461
00:39:34,255 --> 00:39:35,436
[SPEAKER_00]: But here, in reverse,

462
00:39:36,392 --> 00:39:52,045
[SPEAKER_00]: Through that transparency, if you are now sustaining the three kinds of good conduct, in other words, you don't act out of pressure, because you are not willingly choosing to compromise sensory strength, because you are not willingly choosing to stop questioning yourself,

463
00:39:53,438 --> 00:40:09,098
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, those three kinds of conduct would have created a container whereby, basically, you cannot possibly, even if you want to, lose the sight of the peripheral, of the motivation, of the intention behind your actions, of your situation as a whole.

464
00:40:10,277 --> 00:40:17,324
[SPEAKER_00]: Without needing to police it, without needing to chase it, without needing to dissect it, the framework becomes pretty... quite unshakable.

465
00:40:17,985 --> 00:40:19,727
[SPEAKER_00]: Or certainly more unshakable than it was before.

466
00:40:20,408 --> 00:40:25,373
[SPEAKER_00]: And that results in... in basically the four foundations of recollection.

467
00:40:26,871 --> 00:40:51,015
[SPEAKER_00]: Which then, obviously, means if you are, whether sitting, walking, standing, laying down, in regard to body, feeling, in regard to these cornerstones, so to speak, of where people would cover up, would make choices, that are infected with greed, aversion, delusion, if you basically, now, through what we described, cannot lose the sight of that, you are fulfilling the seven awakening factors.

468
00:40:52,096 --> 00:40:56,699
[SPEAKER_00]: So, through the three kinds of good conduct, you are fulfilling seven awakening factors, basically.

469
00:40:58,681 --> 00:41:06,547
[SPEAKER_00]: Fulfillment of satipaṭṭhāna is the result of the three kinds of good conduct, as we did, as we discussed in just previous sutta or two suttas ago.

470
00:41:08,568 --> 00:41:11,570
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the seven awakening factors lead to knowledge and liberation.

471
00:41:13,112 --> 00:41:15,894
[SPEAKER_00]: Again, that's the result of fulfilling them.

472
00:41:17,335 --> 00:41:21,378
[SPEAKER_00]: And this is, again, just goes back to what we speak so often, so many times.

473
00:41:21,398 --> 00:41:21,678
[SPEAKER_00]: It's like

474
00:41:22,752 --> 00:41:25,094
[SPEAKER_00]: There's no special practice.

475
00:41:25,154 --> 00:41:26,495
[SPEAKER_00]: There's no luck involved either.

476
00:41:27,636 --> 00:41:35,722
[SPEAKER_00]: There is certainly no mystical experience that just doing random stuff would come up and then make everything fall into place.

477
00:41:36,683 --> 00:41:39,985
[SPEAKER_00]: It's just training of a layer upon layer upon layer upon layer.

478
00:41:40,606 --> 00:41:45,530
[SPEAKER_00]: And this sutta, so quite quite well, it describes what those layers are.

479
00:41:46,671 --> 00:41:49,473
[SPEAKER_00]: And it describes pretty much why you wouldn't be developing them.

480
00:41:51,054 --> 00:41:52,035
[SPEAKER_00]: As we just addressed it.

481
00:41:54,257 --> 00:41:59,679
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's like the most direct and comprehensive way to enlightenment.

482
00:42:00,819 --> 00:42:05,801
[SPEAKER_00]: It's like there's really nothing else needed, that you need to do, on top of what we just described.

483
00:42:06,461 --> 00:42:18,145
[SPEAKER_00]: And there's nothing that you need to wait for, in a sense, because all these things are always with you, in any, at any given time, in any experience or reason, for whatever it was, whatever it is.

484
00:42:28,631 --> 00:42:32,833
[SPEAKER_00]: So, let's see, we've got a few more questions.

485
00:42:45,998 --> 00:42:52,661
[SPEAKER_00]: Right, so, yeah, I think I kind of already answered it, but yeah, there's a question like, so how do you practice yoniso manasikāra?

486
00:42:53,041 --> 00:42:56,203
[SPEAKER_00]: How do you attend in light of its attention source?

487
00:42:58,371 --> 00:42:59,612
[SPEAKER_00]: The practical example.

488
00:42:59,672 --> 00:43:00,152
[SPEAKER_00]: What do you do?

489
00:43:01,033 --> 00:43:04,555
[SPEAKER_00]: So, do you learn how to shift your attention?

490
00:43:04,595 --> 00:43:05,176
[SPEAKER_00]: How to look?

491
00:43:05,656 --> 00:43:18,585
[SPEAKER_00]: It's like, all of those things, you know, can maybe help you become more informed about it, but fundamentally, you do it by uncovering the intentions behind your actions and behind your attention.

492
00:43:19,106 --> 00:43:20,327
[SPEAKER_00]: Whatever you're choosing to attend,

493
00:43:21,318 --> 00:43:27,701
[SPEAKER_00]: You always need to have a degree of self-awareness revealed through self-questioning as to why am I attending to this?

494
00:43:29,642 --> 00:43:30,683
[SPEAKER_00]: Why am I preferring this?

495
00:43:31,103 --> 00:43:36,966
[SPEAKER_00]: Again, not in order to find some psychological linear first point where these things came up.

496
00:43:37,326 --> 00:43:38,266
[SPEAKER_00]: No, it's right there and then.

497
00:43:38,326 --> 00:43:38,607
[SPEAKER_00]: Why?

498
00:43:39,707 --> 00:43:40,928
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, it's because it's pleasant.

499
00:43:42,268 --> 00:43:44,870
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, it's because it bothers me.

500
00:43:44,930 --> 00:43:46,370
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, it's because of this, because of that.

501
00:43:46,591 --> 00:43:47,091
[SPEAKER_00]: Knowing that.

502
00:43:47,171 --> 00:43:48,571
[SPEAKER_00]: No, you don't need to resolve it.

503
00:43:48,672 --> 00:43:49,272
[SPEAKER_00]: That's the point.

504
00:43:50,188 --> 00:43:51,168
[SPEAKER_00]: There is no resolution.

505
00:43:51,268 --> 00:43:53,649
[SPEAKER_00]: It's just... that's the reason and that's what you're doing.

506
00:43:54,169 --> 00:43:57,331
[SPEAKER_00]: But it's the non... it's the ignoring of that relationship.

507
00:43:59,471 --> 00:44:00,972
[SPEAKER_00]: That's this whole thing we've been describing.

508
00:44:02,132 --> 00:44:05,994
[SPEAKER_00]: And the yoniso manasikāra is the beginning of non-ignoring of that relationship.

509
00:44:07,574 --> 00:44:12,016
[SPEAKER_00]: So that's why, although people might be asking, oh, why am I doing this?

510
00:44:12,036 --> 00:44:12,536
[SPEAKER_00]: Why am I doing that?

511
00:44:12,556 --> 00:44:18,338
[SPEAKER_00]: They're still doing it with another intention behind that action, which is to resolve it.

512
00:44:21,188 --> 00:44:21,848
[SPEAKER_00]: To figure it out.

513
00:44:22,849 --> 00:44:23,429
[SPEAKER_00]: It's like, no.

514
00:44:24,149 --> 00:44:26,450
[SPEAKER_00]: You're doing it only to the extent to reveal it.

515
00:44:26,990 --> 00:44:30,771
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, of course, you don't act out, you don't break the precepts, but you don't try to get rid of it.

516
00:44:31,612 --> 00:44:39,515
[SPEAKER_00]: Because if you try to get rid of it, through this denial, you are getting rid of the relationship of yoniso, which means something else is now yoniso and you're not seeing it.

517
00:44:40,995 --> 00:44:48,358
[SPEAKER_00]: So, questioning, self-questioning that points back to the direction of the source of your current situation, which is going to be

518
00:44:49,254 --> 00:44:54,818
[SPEAKER_00]: As I said, you'll be doing something, thinking something, attending to something, sight, sound, smell, taste, touches, or thoughts.

519
00:44:55,658 --> 00:44:56,519
[SPEAKER_00]: It has to be something.

520
00:44:57,480 --> 00:45:00,462
[SPEAKER_00]: And you always want to have that knowledge as to why.

521
00:45:02,163 --> 00:45:04,204
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, because I prefer it or don't prefer it.

522
00:45:04,224 --> 00:45:04,765
[SPEAKER_00]: I must change it.

523
00:45:04,805 --> 00:45:05,825
[SPEAKER_00]: No, you don't need to change it.

524
00:45:06,526 --> 00:45:08,968
[SPEAKER_00]: You contain it through not acting out and then you reveal it.

525
00:45:09,608 --> 00:45:10,809
[SPEAKER_00]: That's the practice of yoniso.

526
00:45:13,158 --> 00:45:17,622
[SPEAKER_00]: And that, obviously, that practice, if you... the more you question yourself, the easier it will become.

527
00:45:17,902 --> 00:45:22,686
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, you wouldn't even need to go through the, like, formally uttering questions and so on.

528
00:45:22,866 --> 00:45:26,248
[SPEAKER_00]: It would just already become your attitude of just seeing what's behind this.

529
00:45:27,129 --> 00:45:28,270
[SPEAKER_00]: Because you already know where to look.

530
00:45:29,391 --> 00:45:34,535
[SPEAKER_00]: But that's not gonna just magically manifest itself, come, like, fall from the sky.

531
00:45:35,095 --> 00:45:38,098
[SPEAKER_00]: That's the attitude that you will have if you... if you develop it.

532
00:45:38,758 --> 00:45:40,740
[SPEAKER_00]: And if you don't use it, you will lose it, basically.

533
00:45:46,520 --> 00:45:53,684
[SPEAKER_00]: So, then the second question is, look, why does the practice of sati, then why is recollectiveness, why is the non-forgetfulness dependent on this?

534
00:45:56,185 --> 00:45:59,346
[SPEAKER_00]: Dependent on this attitude of discerning the intention behind your actions.

535
00:46:02,388 --> 00:46:07,670
[SPEAKER_00]: Seeing the currently enduring motivation for your attention resting on this or that.

536
00:46:07,890 --> 00:46:09,571
[SPEAKER_00]: Choosing to attend this as opposed to that.

537
00:46:11,573 --> 00:46:13,854
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, we addressed it, didn't we?

538
00:46:13,894 --> 00:46:22,779
[SPEAKER_00]: Basically, the reason why it's because that already gives you the broadest framework of what's important and what should be emphasized.

539
00:46:22,979 --> 00:46:26,241
[SPEAKER_00]: And if you lose the sight of that, then you forget.

540
00:46:28,942 --> 00:46:35,205
[SPEAKER_00]: So, think of it like, basically, say you are at the crossroads and you're not committing to any direction.

541
00:46:35,305 --> 00:46:36,666
[SPEAKER_00]: You stay there on the crossroads.

542
00:46:39,808 --> 00:46:46,334
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, doesn't matter what comes from what side, you will see there, you will recognize it, you won't forget about it.

543
00:46:46,994 --> 00:47:02,888
[SPEAKER_00]: But if you forget about the importance of staying at the crossroads, staying at the origin of this traffic, and you choose one of the lanes, one of the directions, well, you... it will be impossible for you to remember about the other three and what's on them, because you're not there now, seeing it.

544
00:47:04,389 --> 00:47:08,851
[SPEAKER_00]: And what would have passed and taken those directions, you wouldn't see either, because you already narrowed down.

545
00:47:09,472 --> 00:47:13,334
[SPEAKER_00]: You already went down one direction and lost the sight of the other three.

546
00:47:14,514 --> 00:47:15,935
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's why sati depends on it.

547
00:47:18,616 --> 00:47:23,279
[SPEAKER_00]: So, through self-questioning, you develop recollectiveness.

548
00:47:24,499 --> 00:47:26,981
[SPEAKER_00]: You develop the non-losing of the right context.

549
00:47:33,825 --> 00:47:34,665
[SPEAKER_00]: So, yeah.

550
00:47:34,905 --> 00:47:55,252
[SPEAKER_00]: Also, like somebody pointed out that the simile that the Buddha gives, it sort of shows that it's not like... the things are not sequential in a sense that, you know, like the origin of the mountain then fills up the pool and then that pool goes into the stream and that stream goes into the river.

551
00:47:55,352 --> 00:47:55,492
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

552
00:47:55,572 --> 00:47:57,753
[SPEAKER_00]: The origin from the mountain continues filling up.

553
00:47:59,133 --> 00:48:01,334
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the pool is filled up and the water is still coming.

554
00:48:03,162 --> 00:48:05,183
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the streams are filled up and the water is still coming.

555
00:48:05,964 --> 00:48:07,805
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's that common thread that I've been saying.

556
00:48:07,845 --> 00:48:11,507
[SPEAKER_00]: The common thread that starts with your transparency and all the way to knowledge and liberation.

557
00:48:12,167 --> 00:48:13,748
[SPEAKER_00]: That's the water still coming.

558
00:48:15,229 --> 00:48:21,852
[SPEAKER_00]: The effort to uncover the principle, not ignore it, is what will fuel this whole thing, what will fill it up.

559
00:48:21,892 --> 00:48:23,893
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's not just like one, two, three, four, five.

560
00:48:24,354 --> 00:48:28,116
[SPEAKER_00]: It's just one thread intensifying and filling up everything in its way.

561
00:48:35,678 --> 00:48:36,158
[SPEAKER_00]: So...

562
00:48:43,722 --> 00:48:43,882
[SPEAKER_00]: Right.

563
00:48:43,902 --> 00:48:50,205
[SPEAKER_00]: So, then, there's another question, slightly more... Well, it's still kind of related, but...

564
00:48:50,225 --> 00:48:56,128
[SPEAKER_00]: So, yeah, the sutta describes the connection between the five hindrances and the three ways of misconduct.

565
00:48:56,168 --> 00:49:03,412
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the hindrances are fed by the way you act, but hindrances themselves are not actions.

566
00:49:03,763 --> 00:49:04,364
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, that's correct.

567
00:49:04,384 --> 00:49:05,084
[SPEAKER_00]: We already said that.

568
00:49:05,404 --> 00:49:07,326
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, they are basically baits, traps.

569
00:49:08,567 --> 00:49:16,994
[SPEAKER_00]: As the Buddha said in that sutta, the things that arise, that, like, if you don't attend with

570
00:49:17,935 --> 00:49:23,660
[SPEAKER_00]: The knowledge of the source of your attention, while you're attending it, that's how you get hooked, so to speak.

571
00:49:24,320 --> 00:49:28,224
[SPEAKER_00]: So, when he was talking about sensuality, he didn't say that the...

572
00:49:28,364 --> 00:49:30,025
[SPEAKER_00]: I don't know... say beautiful sights.

573
00:49:30,866 --> 00:49:32,567
[SPEAKER_00]: Beautiful sights are not sensuality.

574
00:49:32,988 --> 00:49:33,708
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not a hindrance.

575
00:49:33,908 --> 00:49:38,692
[SPEAKER_00]: People think like, oh, seeing this, smelling this, hearing this, thinking this, that's a hindrance.

576
00:49:38,873 --> 00:49:39,253
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not.

577
00:49:40,432 --> 00:49:44,173
[SPEAKER_00]: That's what comes up, that you don't see through, which is what hinders you.

578
00:49:44,333 --> 00:49:52,015
[SPEAKER_00]: So, technically, it's your lack of yoniso that hinders you in these specific situations.

579
00:49:52,215 --> 00:49:56,557
[SPEAKER_00]: So, when the Buddha said, if the one does not attend rightly, i.e.

580
00:49:56,677 --> 00:50:03,759
[SPEAKER_00]: attend with its source, to the sight of the beauty, that's when sensuality... hindrance of sensuality is there.

581
00:50:04,319 --> 00:50:08,220
[SPEAKER_00]: If he attends to it rightly, then, basically, that thing remains that.

582
00:50:08,951 --> 00:50:12,734
[SPEAKER_00]: The phenomenon of beauty, the sight of beauty remains there.

583
00:50:12,934 --> 00:50:14,255
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the thing has arisen.

584
00:50:14,636 --> 00:50:16,757
[SPEAKER_00]: The bait is still there, but it doesn't hinder you.

585
00:50:18,819 --> 00:50:33,691
[SPEAKER_00]: So, hindrances in themselves are not actions, but it's if you don't see through them, so to speak, if you attend to them without the knowledge of the source while you're attending it, the source of your very attention as to why you're doing it, that's what hinders you.

586
00:50:36,197 --> 00:50:42,580
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's why, like, that whole kind of... the relationship is not something you can just stop.

587
00:50:43,081 --> 00:50:44,741
[SPEAKER_00]: As I said, well, none of this you could just stop.

588
00:50:44,761 --> 00:50:45,502
[SPEAKER_00]: Craving, you can't stop.

589
00:50:45,542 --> 00:50:46,522
[SPEAKER_00]: Hindrances, you can't just stop.

590
00:50:47,183 --> 00:50:54,627
[SPEAKER_00]: But as we just uncovered through the sutta, where the line, where the threshold of your input is, begins and ends.

591
00:50:54,827 --> 00:50:56,187
[SPEAKER_00]: And that is what you're responsible for.

592
00:50:56,227 --> 00:51:00,630
[SPEAKER_00]: And if you stick to that, if you practice that sufficient amount of time, then it will drain away.

593
00:51:01,503 --> 00:51:13,255
[SPEAKER_00]: So then, the sight of the beauty, doesn't matter how many times it will come up for an arahant or an anāgāmī, he's incapable of attending to it without the light of its origin.

594
00:51:14,196 --> 00:51:16,559
[SPEAKER_00]: In other words, he cannot possibly engage in ayoniso.

595
00:51:17,240 --> 00:51:19,422
[SPEAKER_00]: In regard to things that remain standing in the world.

596
00:51:21,004 --> 00:51:27,889
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's another reason why it's important to not just blindly try to get rid of the hindrance and do the opposite and manage it and remove, because you're trying to remove the whole thing.

597
00:51:27,909 --> 00:51:33,453
[SPEAKER_00]: You're picking up the bait, because you say the bait is the problem and I get hooked because this thing is in front of my eyes.

598
00:51:34,154 --> 00:51:36,316
[SPEAKER_00]: No, you get hooked because you grabbed it.

599
00:51:36,376 --> 00:51:38,237
[SPEAKER_00]: It can remain standing in front of your eyes all you want.

600
00:51:38,477 --> 00:51:40,519
[SPEAKER_00]: If you don't reach for it, it can't hook you.

601
00:51:41,379 --> 00:51:42,080
[SPEAKER_00]: As simple as that.

